Usages:
  eager_singleton: |
    Services are registered with the underlying container's eager-singleton scope: the
    container builds the instance once, the first time it is resolved, and returns that same
    instance for every subsequent resolve() call for the same type.

Annotations: |
  This cell is the app-wide dependency-injection boundary: a thin, framework-agnostic contract
  (`ServiceProvider`) plus its one concrete, process-wide implementation (`AppContainer`). Every
  other part of the app that needs a shared service (a logger, a theme manager, a feature
  manager, etc.) registers it here once at startup and resolves it by type afterwards, instead
  of passing it through every initializer by hand.

  Use `eager_singleton` to understand what "registering a service" actually guarantees.

---

"ServiceProvider()":
  location: ServiceProvider.swift
  annotations: |
    Minimal contract any dependency-injection container implementation must satisfy: create the
    container, register services into it, resolve them back out, and reset/bootstrap the whole
    graph. Kept container-framework-agnostic so the concrete backing container library can
    change without changing call sites.
  methods:
    "resolve() -> service:T": |
      Resolve a previously registered service by inferring T from the call site's expected type.
    "resolve(type: T.Type) -> service:T": |
      Resolve a previously registered service by its explicit type.

      `type`: the service type to resolve
    "register(service: T)": |
      Register a service instance under its type T.

      Use `eager_singleton` for how this registration is realized.

      `service`: the instance to register
    "bootstrap()": |
      Resolve the entire registered object graph eagerly, so failures surface at startup rather
      than lazily on first use.
    "reset()": |
      Remove every registered service. Used to rebuild a clean container (e.g. between tests).

"ServiceProvider::AppContainer()":
  location: AppContainer.swift
  annotations: |
    The one concrete `ServiceProvider` used by the running app, exposed as a shared singleton
    instance. Backed internally by a third-party dependency-container library. Consumers never
    construct this type directly; they go through the shared instance.
  properties:
    "shared -> ServiceProvider": |
      The single, process-wide container instance every part of the app registers into and
      resolves from.

